{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:19:11.263Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"The mojibake is just a PowerShell display quirk, nothing to worry about. I should check if next/font/google supports Noto_Sans_Bengali with the Bengali subset, since Geist already built successfully which suggests network access for Google fonts works. Since there's no openai package installed, I'll just use fetch directly for any API calls instead.\n\nI'm sketching out the module structure: an i18n file with Assamese/English UI strings and a translation helper (making sure to avoid the Bengali-only র character in Assamese text), plus a pure report module that groups checks into fix/verify/ok categories, with external checks shown as a visible \"check at the bank\" list, and a helper to identify fields needing confirmation.\n\nI'm also planning a form-to-document converter that takes raw form values and builds an ExtractedDoc per document type, handling things like splitting owner names and extracting the last four Aadhaar digits. I'm working out which fields matter for each doc type, keeping it minimal so each one only tracks the fields the engine actually checks.\n\nFor deciding which document slots to show the user, I'm combining mandatory docs with name-check docs and the name reference, wrapping that into a pure `docSlotsFor(scheme)` function that returns required/optional flags. Next I'm moving to the server-side AI extraction function that takes an image and a document type hint.\n\nI'm sketching how extraction will work: pulling API config from env vars with a fallback error if missing, versioning the prompt, sending the image as a data URL to a chat completions endpoint with JSON response formatting, and validating the model's output against a schema that captures name, DOB, and a masked Aadhaar number — reducing any full Aadhaar number down to just the last four digits for safety before discarding the rest.\n\nI'm also planning resilience details: tolerating nulls from the model, retrying once on parse or network failure before falling back to an error, logging structured events without raw data, injecting fetch and env dependencies for testability, enforcing an image size cap, and defining the overall result type for the extraction function.\n\nNext I'm sketching the API route for extraction: a POST handler accepting a JSON body with the base64 image and optional doc type, validated through zod before calling the extraction logic.\n\nI'm also designing a check route: validate scheme ID, docs, answers, and an optional as-of date, then look up the scheme and evaluate it into a Report, using appropriate status codes (400, 404, 503, 502) and defaulting the date to today in Asia/Kolkata via a small server-side helper.\n\nTo keep routes thin and testable, I plan to move the actual logic into handler functions in a server module, with the route files just calling them. For tests, I'll place them alongside the server code and import the route modules directly, mocking the AI dependency with vi.mock so I can verify behavior without hitting real services.\n\nFor the page itself, since it's a client-heavy flow, I need to decide how scheme data reaches the client component -- either the server page fetches and passes serializable scheme summaries as props, or the client imports the pure core modules directly since they're bundleable. The simpler path is probably having the client pull in the core logic directly for scheme list, questions, doc slots, and labels.\n\nFor UI structure, I'm planning page.tsx as a server component rendering a client CheckFlow component, housed in a private `_components` folder, with CheckFlow managing a step-based state machine across scheme selection, docs, confirmation, questions, and the final report.\n\nI also want a separate ReportView component for rendering the report with a print button, plus a small shared UI module for banners and buttons. The language toggle will live in CheckFlow's state and sync to `document.documentElement.lang`. For the scheme step, I'm showing three cards with bilingual names and a flagship badge for PM-KISAN, while sample documents (A/B/C) are tied to the docs step and filtered by scheme—A and B for PM-KISAN, C for the widow pension scheme.\n\nEach document slot will be its own card showing status plus \"Take photo,\" \"Type details,\" and \"I don't have this\" options, where photo capture reads the file, sends it to the extraction API, and falls back to manual entry if extraction fails. Sample buttons at the top of the docs step will preload sample data and jump straight to the confirmation step with a demo banner.\n\nFor the photo input, I'll avoid forcing the camera open directly—using plain `accept=\"image/*\"` instead of a capture attribute—so users can pick gallery or camera, keeping things less intrusive while still fulfilling the \"photo per mandatory doc\" requirement. I'll also downscale large phone photos client-side via canvas to a max width before converting to base64, to keep upload sizes manageable.\n\nLow-confidence fields (below 0.8) get highlighted amber with a \"please check\" prompt and a \"✓ Correct\" button; editing or clicking it clears the flag, while unconfirmed low-confidence fields stay flagged for the engine's verification step. Aadhaar numbers are masked except the last four digits, which the user re-enters to confirm. Since the sample data all has confidence ≥0.9, nothing gets highlighted in the demo. Next I'm setting up the questions section with large Yes/No buttons per scheme question.\n\nI'm also adding a readiness banner summary line noting how many items need confirming at the bank, linking down to that section. For benefits, I'll show central share and state top-up separately, with a fallback message when state top-up isn't determined, formatting currency with en-IN digits for clarity in the Assamese UI.\n\nItems are grouped into \"Fix before you apply,\" \"Please verify,\" and collapsed \"OK\" sections, each showing the message, location, source link with verification date, and a provisional-rule badge when relevant. I'm also adding a supporting docs list for Orunodoi, scheme version info, and a print button with print-specific CSS to hide controls and produce a clean cover sheet.\n\nFor fonts, I'm planning to keep Geist for Latin text and add Noto Sans Bengali via next/font/google, checking that the subset is available and weighing the risk that build-time font fetching could fail offline — though it's worked fine before.\n\nOn styling, I realize globals.css currently forces Arial on the body, which would override my font stack, so I need to set the body to use a proper font-family variable that falls back through Geist then Noto Bengali then sans-serif, relying on per-glyph fallback across the list. For color scheme, I'm leaning toward sidestepping the existing dark mode variables entirely and just using explicit light colors like white background and dark zinc text so cards don't clash with a potentially dark body.\n\nI'm now sketching out the bilingual i18n approach: a Lang type for Assamese and English, a UI dictionary mapping keys to bilingual strings, a `t(lang, key)` translator, and a `pick`/`byLang` helper for objects that store language-suffixed fields like messageEn/messageAs. For strings needing interpolation, I'm planning a simple template-based `fmt` or `t(lang, key, vars)` function that substitutes placeholders.\n\nFor the Assamese strings themselves, I need to be careful with letterforms — using ৰ and ৱ correctly and avoiding the Bengali-specific র, while confirming ব is valid. I'm drafting labels like app title, language toggle button text, step headings, and scheme names (e.g. \"আঁচনি বাছক\" for \"Choose a scheme\" and \"মূল ডেমো\" for \"Flagship\") in both languages.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining step labels and strings — naming steps for documents, confirmation, questions, and results, plus writing help text for the documents screen like \"Take a photo of each document, or type the details yourself\" in both Assamese and English.\n\nContinuing with required, optional, remove, and aiUnavailable labels in Assamese and English, covering mandatory documents, name-check items, and the fallback message when automatic reading isn't available.\n\nAdding the failed-read message, a note that photos aren't stored, navigation labels for next and back, and starting the confirmation screen title for the extracted data.\n\nContinuing with more field labels like name, date of birth, Aadhaar digits, account number, IFSC code, and land owner names, each with Assamese and English translations.\n\nContinuing with yes/no labels, result-checking states, and labels for eligibility and application readiness sections, all in Assamese paired with English translations.\n\nNow I'm adding verdict labels like likely eligible, not eligible, needs verification, ready for review, and action required, plus a summary string for external verification steps with banks or offices.\n\nI'm also drafting the fix and verify section headers, the external verification subsection, the OK count display, the \"where to fix\" label, and starting the source field in both Assamese and English.\n\nAdding more translation keys...\n\nAdding docForNameCheck label and the cover sheet title using the app title, plus reusing the existing disclaimer wording. I'm also mapping verdict tones in core/report.ts so likely_eligible and ready_for_review map to green.\n\nI'll verify a few remaining details before coding: whether `next/font/google` includes Noto Sans Bengali for Assamese rendering, Tailwind's `print:` variant support, and the current baseline test state.\n\n"}}
