{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:20:35.970Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"With 93 baseline tests passing, I'm laying out a plan: first a pure i18n module with Assamese/English translations and a test ensuring Assamese strings use the correct script characters, then an AI server module for document extraction from images.\n\nThis ai.ts module reads API credentials from env, calls an external chat completions endpoint with an image data URI requesting JSON output, validates the response against a zod schema, masks sensitive fields like Aadhaar numbers to last four digits, retries once on failure, and falls back to a safe error object while logging the model and prompt version. I want fetch injectable so tests can mock it without hitting the network, and the raw-to-extracted conversion function should be pure and separately testable, following the rule that all AI calls funnel through one module with schema validation and fallback.\n\nThen I'm sketching thin API routes for extract and check endpoints, testable in a Node environment by importing the route handlers directly and mocking server dependencies.\n\nI'm deciding to colocate route tests under src/app/api, confirming Next won't treat non-special-named files there as routes, and settling on importing route modules from a central test file. For the check route, I'm defining the request validation schema (schemeId, docs, answers, optional asOfDate), with the server computing today's date in Asia/Kolkata timezone via a helper when not provided, and mapping errors to 400 for invalid input and 404 for unknown schemes.\n\nFor scheme metadata, I'm weighing whether to have a server component page fetch the scheme list and pass a serialized summary down to a client component, versus importing the registry directly client-side, and leaning toward a minimal SchemeSummary shape with id, version, names, mandatory docs, and questions.\n\nRather than adding an API route, I'll just pass data as props from the server page, including samples, keeping the client bundle lean while leaving room for T3 to make it dynamic later. I also need to define a pure module mapping each document type to the fields it should display — like name/dob/aadhaar digits for Aadhaar, account details for bank passbooks, owner names for land records, and so on for the remaining doc types.\n\nFor manual entry when AI is unavailable, I'll treat user-confirmed fields as having no confidence score, and write pure functions like `confirmField` and `lowConfidenceFields` with tests to handle confirming/editing low-confidence values, plus form conversions like turning comma-separated owner names into an array and validating that Aadhaar entries are exactly 4 digits.\n\nFor the report grouping logic, I'm designing a `groupChecks` function that buckets checks into fix/verify/ok categories, and wrestling with where \"not_applicable\" results (like a missing document preventing a name comparison) should land—leaning toward not lumping them into the collapsed OK group since that could be misleading.\n\nWithin the \"please verify\" group, I'm reconciling two slightly conflicting requirements: showing external checks as a visible \"check at the bank\" list under the readiness banner, while also including them under \"please verify.\" I'm settling on splitting verify into two subgroups—regular items first, then a \"confirm at bank/office\" subsection for external checks—to satisfy both without duplication.\n\nI need to pull external check labels from the scheme's externalChecks data since Check objects only have messages, not standalone labels. For the sample readiness checks, the mismatch and bank-match messages should name the specific land record and bank variant. For benefits display, I'll show central and state shares separately, falling back to \"confirm locally\" when the state top-up is unspecified.\n\nFor currency formatting, I'm deciding between using Assamese-locale digit formatting versus standard en-IN formatting, since Assamese scheme text traditionally uses native digit glyphs for rupee amounts.\n\nRather than relying on ICU locale behavior (which could cause hydration mismatches between server and client), I'll format numbers with en-IN then build a deterministic digit mapper that converts 0-9 to the Assamese numeral glyphs when needed, applying the same approach to dates. For the printable cover sheet, I'm planning print-specific CSS that hides interactive controls and reveals a clean summary block with scheme name, verification date, verdicts, and applicant details when the user triggers printing.\n\nI'll default the language to Assamese, toggle it via state, and sync it to the document's lang attribute through an effect. For typography, since the build environment has network access to fetch Google Fonts (confirmed by the existing Geist setup), I'm adding Noto Sans Bengali alongside the current font and updating the body to use the combined font variable instead of the current Arial fallback.\n\nFor photo uploads, I'm using a plain file input without the camera capture attribute so users can choose gallery or camera themselves, avoiding unnecessary camera data collection. On the client side I'm adding a canvas-based helper to downscale images to a max 1600px JPEG before converting to base64, keeping payloads small, with the server rejecting anything oversized as a backstop.\n\nNow I'm designing the extraction endpoint, which takes an image and returns parsed document data or an error, with a manual entry fallback always available so users can type details themselves if OCR fails or no API key is configured. I'm mapping out the overall flow states—scheme selection, document upload, confirmation, questions, and report—and thinking through how sample documents should be filtered by scheme on the docs step.\n\nI'm also adding a \"Try a demo\" option on the scheme step that preloads a scheme, sample docs, and sample answers all at once, jumping straight to the confirm step with a persistent demo banner that stays until the user restarts. Sample documents will have high confidence scores so they won't trigger the low-confidence warning UI.\n\nFor the confirm step, low-confidence fields (<0.8) get a \"please check\" marker and a confirm button to clear the flag, while editing a field also clears its confidence automatically — users can still proceed even with unconfirmed fields, since the engine will flag them as needing verification. Users can also skip uploading a document entirely (like sample C missing a death certificate), and the continue button stays enabled regardless, letting the engine handle missing-document flags. I'm also planning a lightweight AI logging format that records only the model, prompt version, input hash, and outcome, with no personal data. Now I'm starting to sketch out the AI module itself.\n\nI'm normalizing the image input to always have a proper data URL prefix, then validating the base64 content. For the model's output, I'm defining a schema with optional fields like docType, name, dob, Aadhaar number, bank account details, and confidence scores, all treated as nullable since extraction may be incomplete.\n\nFor converting raw extraction into the final document, I'm masking Aadhaar numbers down to the last four digits and stripping any stray 12-digit sequences that might leak into other fields, dropping empty strings along the way. I still need to check how dates of birth should be normalized since I'm not sure what format the age-check logic expects, so I want to look at that validation code more closely.\n\nMissing confidence values from the model should default to a low score since that signals the field hasn't actually been confirmed, and I should carry that same confidence logic over to the derived Aadhaar-last-4 field. For mismatches between the detected document type and the upload slot's expected type, I'll keep the model's detected type but let the UI layer decide whether to override it or just surface a warning to the user.\n\nFor resilience, I'll retry failed AI calls once on network errors, bad status codes, JSON parsing issues, or schema validation failures, falling back to an explicit error result afterward, with a 30-second timeout enforced via an abort signal. Since I'm unsure whether the Cline API reliably supports structured JSON response formatting, I'm leaning toward just instructing the model via prompt to return pure JSON and then extracting the first JSON object from the raw text rather than relying on a response_format parameter that might not be supported.\n\nI also need to define the request schema for the route itself, validating the incoming docs array strictly against the extracted-doc shape, along with scheme id, optional version, answers, and an optional as-of date, defaulting the date to today in the Asia/Kolkata timezone.\n\nFor the clock utility, I'm writing an injectable now-function so tests can control the date, formatting it as YYYY-MM-DD via Intl.DateTimeFormat. Since POST handlers aren't prerendered under cache components, using the real current time there is safe.\n\nThe main page is a server component that builds scheme summaries from the pure listSchemes function and sample data, passing them into a client-side CheckFlow component — this works fine at build time since listSchemes is pure. I'm also starting to sketch out the core flow module, defining the document field mappings and beginning the low-confidence handling logic.\n\nStill defining core flow functions...\n\nNow I'm planning the i18n setup — a language type restricted to Assamese and English, with the translation file living at the project root per spec.\n\nI should verify labels.ts for that character too, assuming the earlier test covers it. For verdict icons, I'm mapping eligibility and readiness statuses to colored circles (green/red/yellow/white) plus accompanying text labels for accessibility rather than relying on color alone. For each check item, I'm planning to show the message, a \"where to fix\" note, a source link (or a \"pending\" placeholder when missing), a verified-on date, and a provisional-rule badge when applicable.\n\nI'm also thinking through what else the report needs: benefit details, supporting documents like Orunodoi, a disclaimer footer, scheme version and as-of date, plus \"print cover sheet\" and \"start over\" actions — no AI-generated Assamese explainer needed here. I want to double check the date-of-birth format used in the age check logic, then plan how to split the large client component into smaller pieces under `src/app/_components/`, including a flow controller and separate step components for scheme selection, documents, confirmation, questions, and the report/cover sheet views.\n\nFor the shared UI bits, I'll keep them in that same private folder so Next.js excludes it from routing, and I need to be careful with the newer React Compiler lint rules in eslint-config-next 16 that flag calling setState inside effects. For testing, I'm outlining API test cases that mock the AI module and verify each sample scenario's expected statuses and messages — ready-for-review versus action-required cases with specific document messages, manual entry reports, invalid input returning 400, unknown scheme returning 404, and AI-unavailable returning 503.\n\nI'm also planning ai.test.ts coverage: missing API key should short-circuit to ai_unavailable without calling fetch, successful mocked fetch should mask Aadhaar numbers down to the last four digits with no full number leaking anywhere in the JSON, confidence should default when missing, bad JSON should trigger one retry before succeeding, and two consecutive failures should produce ai_failed with fetch called twice — plus logs should capture model, prompt version, and input hash but never the raw image.\n\nI'm deciding to avoid importing next/server in route modules and just use plain Response.json so tests run cleanly in node environment, confirming vitest resolves the @/ alias, and following test-first order — writing failing tests before implementation, starting with checking the dob format logic for the age check.\n\n"}}
