{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:25:20.089Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm planning the server-side AI module that isolates the model-calling logic into a single function for extracting document data from an image, handling the API key and model config from environment variables, gracefully falling back when unavailable, and calling the chat completions endpoint with structured output requested.\n\nI'm also thinking through validation: parsing the response against a schema, masking the Aadhaar number down to its last four digits for privacy, retrying once on invalid output before falling back to an error, and logging only non-personal metadata like model name and input hash. I want the normalization logic — converting raw AI output into the final extracted document shape — to live in a separate pure, testable module rather than mixed into the network-calling code.\n\nNow I'm sketching the API route for document extraction: accepting a base64 image with an optional doc type, enforcing a size limit, calling the extraction function, and deciding on response semantics (returning a success doc or an error, with the UI falling back to manual entry regardless of the HTTP status used).\n\nFor the check endpoint, I'm planning a POST accepting scheme ID, version, docs, answers, and an optional as-of date, with the server computing \"today\" in Asia/Kolkata time outside the core logic. I'm also weighing whether a schemes listing endpoint is needed, or whether the UI can just import the pure scheme registry modules directly since they're client-safe.\n\nSince the registry gets populated at runtime on the server, a client-side import could go stale, so I'll have the server page component build scheme summaries and pass them as props to a client component, keeping things simple with cacheComponents handling staleness later. For testing, I'll keep handler logic directly in route.ts and mock the ai module to test the exported POST function.\n\nI'll avoid Next-specific imports in route handlers by using plain Response.json so tests run cleanly in a node environment, and I'll colocate test files like route.test.ts alongside route.ts since only specific filenames like route.ts and page.tsx are treated specially by the app router.\n\nFor i18n, I'm setting up a Lang type for Assamese and English with parallel STRINGS dictionaries, reusing the doc labels via a helper, and writing a test to verify every English key exists non-empty in Assamese and that Assamese text correctly uses the ৰ character rather than the Bengali র, since Assamese never uses that glyph.\n\nFor the footer, I want to reuse the existing disclaimer text from the report module and show both Assamese and English versions together.\n\nNext I plan a reportView module in core that groups checks into fix/verify/ok sections and maps verdict tones, building it test-first, and I'm considering whether a manualEntry module is needed for the confirm-fields step.\n\nI need a mapping of which fields to show per document type for the confirm step, plus helpers to determine if a field needs confirmation based on confidence, which documents a scheme requires, and how editing a field should clear its confidence flag -- though I still need to figure out how a user confirms a low-confidence field without changing its value.\n\nI'll design it so confirming a field explicitly clears its confidence marker, while unconfirmed low-confidence fields stay flagged so the engine correctly requires verification. I'll implement pure `setField` and `confirmField` functions in a core `docEdit.ts` module and test them.\n\nFor field sets per document type, I'll keep things minimal: aadhaar needs name/dob/last-4-digits, bank needs name/account/IFSC, land needs owner names, ration card needs name, and death certificate/BPL proof just need presence since no rule actually inspects their specific fields.\n\nFor photo capture, I'm using a file input with camera access, reading via FileReader as base64, then downscaling through a canvas to a reasonable JPEG size before posting to the extraction endpoint—important since raw phone photos can be several megabytes.\n\nIf extraction fails, I'll fall back to manual entry with empty fields and a message explaining automatic reading didn't work; images are never persisted, just discarded from client state once extraction completes.\n\nFor the scheme documents step, I'm listing mandatory documents plus any docs used in name-matching checks (like Aadhaar and bank for Orunodoi) as the primary focused list, skipping less relevant supporting documents to avoid clutter.\n\nEach doc slot should also support \"Add manually\" (in case AI extraction isn't available) and \"Remove\" options. For demo convenience, I'll add \"Use sample A/B/C\" buttons scoped to the selected scheme (A and B for PM-KISAN, C for the widow scheme), and also place quick-start sample buttons on the scheme selection step itself so users can jump straight into a populated flow.\n\nUsing a sample should pre-fill both the documents and the questions step answers, and trigger a persistent \"Demo mode\" banner shown on every screen including the final report. The questions step itself should use large Yes/No buttons per scheme's question set, pre-filled from sample data but still allowing unanswered questions (which the engine treats as needing verification), and the report step just posts to the check API and renders the resulting report.\n\nFor the report rendering, I'm mapping eligibility and readiness states to colored indicators (green/red/yellow/white), with white reserved for unknown or not-yet-checked states, and surfacing a dedicated \"check at the bank\" list for anything flagged as needing verification.\n\nI need to avoid duplicating external checks between the readiness banner and the \"please verify\" section, so I'm planning a compact summary under the banner (counts/short labels) with full detail in the verify section below.\n\nI'll pull labels from the scheme summary's external checks list, accepting the slight duplication since both views were explicitly requested. Each item should show its message, where to fix it, a source link with verification date, and a provisional badge when the provenance status calls for it, while the benefit breakdown should separate central share and state top-up amounts, only totaling them when both are known.\n\nFor number formatting, I'm standardizing on Indian-style grouping with Latin digits for both languages to keep things simple and readable, and I'll add a supporting documents section plus a print-only cover sheet that hides the interactive controls.\n\nI'm also planning how OK items expand in print view since collapsed details won't print, so I'll drive visibility with my own toggle state and a print-specific class instead. I want a bilingual disclaimer in the footer, a language toggle button that updates the document's lang attribute, and I'm adding a Bengali-script font alongside Geist to support both languages properly.\n\nChecking the build confirms Google Fonts are reachable, so I'll set the body font stack to use the Bengali font first for consistency, falling back to Geist. For theming, I'm leaning toward a mostly light design with explicit neutral colors rather than relying on automatic dark mode, since that keeps the verdict banners' contrast predictable.\n\nI'll compute the asOfDate server-side using Kolkata timezone formatting, placed in a small server utility, since the UI doesn't need to pass a date and the sample widow-age calculation still works fine with today's date.\n\nFor the AI module, I'm sketching out its types and error states: an AiError union covering unavailable/failed/bad-input cases, an ExtractResult that's either a successful doc with model name or an error, and an AiDeps interface for injecting fetch/env/logging so the extraction function stays testable.\n\nI'm handling image input flexibly, accepting either a data URL or raw base64 and inferring mime type, then validating size and character constraints before sending it off. The request builds a chat completion call with a system prompt and image content, forcing JSON response format at zero temperature, then parses the model's reply by stripping markdown fences and validating against a raw extraction schema.\n\nFor Aadhaar numbers specifically, I'm being defensive about privacy — only keeping the last four digits regardless of what field the model puts it in, and masking before any logging happens. For resolving document type mismatches between the model's guess and the user's hint, I'm leaning toward trusting the model unless it says \"other,\" in which case fall back to the hint, though I need to think through what happens if someone uploads the wrong document type entirely.\n\nFor dates of birth, I'll normalize to YYYY-MM-DD in a pure helper function, converting formats like DD/MM/YYYY, and if parsing fails I'll just keep the raw string with a low confidence score rather than failing outright.\n\nFor safety, any AI-extracted field that's present but lacks an explicit confidence score should default to 0.5 rather than being treated as confirmed — missing confidence shouldn't silently mean \"trusted.\" I also want retry logic: up to two attempts on network errors, bad status codes, or JSON/schema failures (but not for missing API keys), with a 30-second timeout via AbortSignal, plus structured logging of each AI call including model, prompt version, and input hash.\n\nI considered adding an availability check endpoint so the UI could preemptively warn users when the AI reader is off, but decided it's unnecessary since upload errors already surface that. For testing, I'm planning coverage for extraction logic (masking, confidence defaults, date normalization, name handling, type validation) and flow logic around document slots and fields.\n\nI'm also planning tests for the grouped report view checking order and verdict tones across sample cases, i18n key parity, and the AI server and API routes with mocked fetch calls, so the whole pipeline is verified end-to-end when AI is unavailable.\n\nNow I'm drafting the Assamese translation strings for the UI, carefully using the correct script for app name, tagline, and language toggle labels.\n\nStill adding translation pairs...\n\nAdding more translation strings...\n\nStill writing translation strings...\n\nContinuing with more field labels like account number, IFSC code, owner names, and husband's name, plus an Aadhaar privacy hint and intro text guiding users to answer for their family and skip unsure items.\n\nAdding translations for eligibility verdicts and readiness statuses, covering likely eligible, not eligible, needs verification, and ready-to-apply states.\n\nI'm continuing to draft the remaining verdict explanation strings for ready_for_review, action_required, and needs_verification states in both English and Assamese, along with starting the externalIntro text for additional confirmations.\n\nStill writing translation strings...\n\nI also need labels for the cover sheet title and a draft note explaining Assamese text is unreviewed, shown small in the footer alongside the disclaimer in both languages. Now I'm moving to the core flow module, starting with the DocSlot interface definition.\n\nStill writing flow helpers...\n\nI'm realizing there's a UX issue with aadhaarLast4: since the field only stores exactly 4 digits, a controlled input bound directly to the field value would clear itself while the user is still typing fewer digits. I need to keep a local draft state for that input, limited to 4 numeric characters, and only commit to the real field once it's valid.\n\nI'm also noting a similar issue for DOB — if the AI extraction gave a non-ISO date, a native date input will just render empty since it requires YYYY-MM-DD format.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the reportView module: defining tone types (green/red/yellow/grey) with emoji mappings, a verdict-to-tone lookup, and a sections structure grouping checks into fix/verify/ok/external categories.\n\nDeciding that not_applicable checks belong in a collapsed \"OK\" section rather than fix or verify, and ordering verify items so non-external checks come before external ones. I'm also adding helper functions for formatting currency like \"₹6,000\" and checking if a check is provisional, plus noting that needs_verification should map to yellow tone across both Eligibility and Readiness.\n\nFor passing scheme data from server to client, I'm considering just passing the full SchemeRule list as a serializable prop and letting the client import pure helpers like questionsFor directly — though that triggers the scheme registry to re-register on the client side too, which is a minor duplication but acceptable.\n\nThe page will pass `schemes={listSchemes()}` and `samples` as props, keeping things static-friendly with cacheComponents. I'm confirming the scheme ordering matches insertion order with PM-KISAN first, then thinking through what the client's report request payload should look like.\n\nThe route needs zod validation for schemeId, optional version, a bounded docs array, answers, and an optional asOfDate, with 404 handling when the scheme/version isn't found. I also need to check body size limits for base64 images since the client downsizes photos to roughly 300-600KB, and make sure the report screen properly surfaces benefit override notes along with print-specific Tailwind styling.\n\nNow I'm planning the component structure: the layout stays a server component for fonts while a client component handles language toggling and updates the document's lang attribute. The main CheckFlow component manages a step state machine (scheme, docs, confirm, questions, report) along with language, scheme ID, documents, answers, and loading state, and I'm splitting it into separate files like SchemeStep and DocsStep to stay within editor size limits.\n\nRounding out the file structure, I'm adding ConfirmStep, QuestionsStep, ReportView, and a shared ui.tsx for buttons and headers, all placed in a private `_components` folder. I also need a client-side helper to downscale and convert photos to JPEG data URLs using canvas before uploading, and I'm now considering how eslint-config-next's React Compiler lint rules might affect this setup.\n\nNow I'm drafting the system prompt for document extraction, specifying it should return a strict JSON object with docType, fields, and confidence, where fields capture the name as printed, date of birth in ISO format, and a partial Aadhaar number.\n\nI'm also thinking through the API call setup — using the Cline chat completions endpoint with a bearer token auth header, zero temperature, a max token cap, and a retry-once strategy if the API rejects the images.\n\nI'm weighing whether to include response_format as json_object since support varies by provider; to stay safe, I'll try including it on the first attempt but fall back to dropping it if that call returns a 400, relying on prompt instructions plus fence-stripping and zod validation to enforce structure either way.\n\nNow I'm drafting the extract route tests, mocking the AI module to check that an unavailable error returns 503, a successful extraction returns 200, and an invalid body returns 400 before ever calling the AI. Writing the route handler...\n\nI'll keep the image size limit in the pure extraction module instead, exporting it as a constant there so it's importable without pulling in the AI mock. Then I'll plan the test files to write first—extraction, flow, reportView, i18n, ai, and route—before building out the actual implementations.\n\nStill writing schema...\n\nI'm handling both possible confidence key names for Aadhaar, splitting owner names on newlines/semicolons/commas, and falling back docType detection to \"other\" with a final schema validation pass. I'm also planning a property test to ensure the Aadhaar number never leaks into the output JSON beyond its last four digits, then starting on the flow.ts types for editable fields.\n\nNow I'm mapping each document type to its set of editable fields and defining a DocSlot interface with required flags, followed by a function to determine which document slots apply for a given scheme.\n\nDefining document slots per scheme...\n\nI also need to think about data minimization — only keeping fields relevant to the scheme's doc type, since the AI extraction might return extra personal data like husbandName that isn't needed. I'm debating whether to move the field whitelist definitions into the extraction module itself so pruning can happen right at extraction time rather than relying on the flow module.\n\nFor setField parsing, I'm planning simple normalization rules: trimming text fields and deleting them when empty, keeping only the last 4 digits for Aadhaar, splitting owner names on common delimiters, and always clearing any stale confidence scores when a field changes.\n\nFor reportView, I'm sketching a helper that partitions a report into fix/verify/ok/external sections, where verify includes external items at the end but external also needs its own separate list for the readiness banner.\n\nI'm planning tests across samples A, B, and C to check that each section groups the right fields correctly, and separately I need a currency formatter for Indian-style number grouping (like ₹6,000 and ₹1,25,000) — I'll write this manually rather than relying on locale APIs to keep it deterministic.\n\nFor the i18n module, I'm setting up language dictionaries for English and Assamese, with helper functions to pull localized strings, document labels, and fix-where labels based on the current language.\n\nI need labels for eligibility and readiness states, with separate notes per status. I'm being careful to use the correct Assamese \"ৰ\" character consistently rather than the Bengali variant, since tests check for this. Now I'm starting on the ai.ts module, the only one permitted to call a model, setting up imports for hashing and extraction utilities.\n\nWriting API constants...\n\nWriting extraction function logic...\n\nAfter getting the model response, I parse out the JSON, validate it against the raw extraction schema, convert it to the final document shape, log success, and return it. On failure I log a sanitized error kind instead of the raw message to avoid leaking personal data, distinguishing schema, JSON, HTTP, timeout, and network errors, then retry before ultimately failing.\n\nI'm also sketching tests that mock fetch to simulate missing API keys, batch model rejection, and successful responses where I verify Aadhaar numbers get masked down to their last four digits in the output.\n\nBeyond that, I need tests covering retry behavior on invalid JSON, repeated failures leading to ai_failed, fetch throwing exceptions, bad base64 input rejection, and fenced JSON parsing. For the route layer, I'm planning tests for valid document submissions, unknown schemes returning 404, malformed bodies and dates returning 400, and the manual-entry flow working end to end.\n\nI'm confirming that using todayInIndia() inside a POST handler is safe under cacheComponents since dynamic rendering only concerns GET routes during prerendering. Now I'm shifting to building the CheckFlow UI component, starting with its step type definitions for scheme, docs, confirm, questions, and report stages.\n\nWriting component state hooks...\n\nI'm adding a runCheck function that posts to the check API and navigates to the report, plus a startOver that resets everything back to the scheme step. I'm thinking through edge cases like whether the demo flag should persist after editing sample docs, deciding to keep it simple and only clear sampleId on scheme change or reset. Then I'm sketching the layout: a header with app name, language toggle, and step indicator, a demo banner when sample data is active, and a constrained main content area with large touch targets, followed by the scheme selection cards showing localized names and a flagship badge.\n\nFor the scheme cards I'm deciding to skip the benefit line to avoid clutter, just showing the name in the current language with the other-language name smaller and a flagship badge, and I'm noting a flagship scheme ID constant should live in the flow config. Now I'm working out the props needed for the docs step component — scheme, docs, setter, samples, sample handler, language, and navigation callbacks — along with per-slot card details like title, required badge, and status.\n\nFor each document slot I'm adding buttons to take or upload a photo versus typing details manually, which adds a blank doc for later confirmation, plus a remove option, and I'm tracking reading/loading state and messages per document type. I'm writing the photo upload handler that converts the image to a data URL, sends it to the extraction API, and merges the returned document data back into state if successful.\n\nOn mismatched or failed extraction it falls back to inserting a blank doc and shows an appropriate error message, and since the docs state closes over a stale value after the await, I need to use functional setState updates instead. Messages should be keyed by an enum rather than raw strings so language switching properly re-renders the UI.\n\nFor demo purposes I'll add a compact \"try a fake sample\" box with buttons at the top of the docs step, styled with a dashed border. The ConfirmStep renders a card per document with its fields, highlighting low-confidence ones in yellow with a \"please check\" prompt and confirm button, handling empty-field and empty-docs cases before letting the user proceed to questions.\n\nI'm building a FieldInput component that manages its own text state and determines whether a field needs confirmation highlighting, with input types tailored per field (date pickers, numeric inputs for account numbers and codes, textareas for names), but I'm realizing there's a bug where capturing the doc object in closures could cause stale updates when multiple fields change in quick succession.\n\nTo fix this properly, I'll switch the parent's onChange handler to use a functional state update that maps over the docs array and applies the field change to the matching document, avoiding the stale closure issue entirely. Then I'm sketching out the QuestionsStep flow with Yes/No/Not sure buttons per question, functional state updates for answers, and a \"See my report\" button that triggers the check, followed by the ReportView component with its sections, print button, and a minimal print-only header for the cover sheet.\n\nNow for each result item, I'm rendering the localized message, a \"where to fix\" note when applicable, a source line linking to the verification URL with a verified-on date and provisional badge, and optionally a clause snippet. For external checklist items I'm pulling labels from the scheme's externalChecks list. The readiness banner uses tone-based color classes (green/red/amber/grey) with matching text and emoji, and the benefit section shows the central share, state top-up (or a \"confirm locally\" placeholder), total amount, instalment count, and any override notes.\n\nI'm also adding supporting docs display, a bilingual disclaimer footer on the report, and planning to simplify the font setup to just Noto Sans Bengali (dropping Geist) for consistent light-mode rendering. Now I'm starting the server page component that wires in the CheckFlow.\n\nI need to make sure the SchemeRule data passed to the client component stays serializable, and double-check that the client-side imports (schemes registry, flow logic, i18n, zod types) work cleanly in the browser bundle. Before going further, I want to write the tests first.\n\nWith 93/93 tests passing as baseline, I've outlined the T2 plan covering core extraction/flow/report logic, i18n, server AI handling and API routes, and the mobile UI flow — now writing the tests first.\n\n"}}
