{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:27:26.652Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I realize I should lay out my plan before diving into tests: I'll build an extraction module with a lenient raw schema, a converter to the final extracted document shape, a date-of-birth normalizer, and a default confidence constant that the AI module will consume.\n\nContinuing the plan, I'll add a flow module handling document slots and fields, confirmation state, and related CRUD helpers for documents; a report view module for sections, tone, and formatting; an i18n file; a server-side AI extraction function with injectable fetch, zod validation, retry logic, and graceful handling of missing API keys; pure API handler functions for extract and check that can be tested with mocked AI; Next.js route files wrapping those handlers using plain Response objects (avoiding next/server dependencies in tests); and a client-rendered app page. For testing, I'll mock the AI module and hit the route handlers directly with constructed Request objects, and make sure the flow logic correctly scopes required document slots per scheme like PM-KISAN.\n\nI'm now reasoning through type details: DOC_FIELDS should be a record keyed by document type mapping to field names, and vitest's `toContain` matcher is generic enough to avoid type errors for strings outside that union. For extraction testing, I need RawExtraction to tolerate arbitrary doc type strings, numeric values getting coerced to strings, semicolon/newline-separated name lists, and a confidence schema allowing values above 1 that then get clamped down during processing, with low-confidence fields like a four-digit DOB forced below a threshold. I'm also checking that field extraction correctly drops irrelevant fields depending on document type, like land records only keeping owner names.\n\nI'm now defining which fields belong to each document type—aadhaar keeps name, dob, and last four digits; bank passbook keeps name, account number, and IFSC; land records keep owner names; ration cards keep name; and I'm debating whether death certificates should retain the husband's name as a stand-in for the deceased's name, since the engine doesn't actually validate names on that document type.\n\nI'll settle the minimal field sets for each document type, giving bpl_proof a name field and leaving others with sensible minimum slots like name and dob where relevant. For AI-read fields lacking confidence, I'll set DEFAULT_AI_CONFIDENCE to 0.5 so they're still low enough to require user confirmation rather than being auto-treated as confirmed, and I'll handle aadhaarLast4 by extracting the last four digits from a longer raw string.\n\nI'm defining docType detection logic — using the provided hint when available, otherwise checking if the raw docType matches the enum and falling back to \"other\" if not. For the schema, I'm making fields and confidence lenient records that tolerate unknown or invalid values, filtering for actual numbers in code rather than relying on strict zod validation to avoid parse failures on malformed input.\n\nFor the report view, I'm verifying that sectionsOf correctly buckets every check into exactly one section (fix, verify, ok, or external) with no overlaps, and that toneOf maps status categories to colors — green for eligible/ready states, red for action-required/not-eligible, yellow for needs-verification.\n\nI'm implementing formatInr manually with Indian-style digit grouping rather than relying on Intl, since ICU behavior can vary — and building the benefitView shape covering central/state top-up amounts, totals, instalments, and override notes, including the PM-KISAN zero-rupee case.\n\nFor i18n, I need STRINGS.en and STRINGS.as to share identical keys with non-empty outputs for both plain strings and label functions, while making sure the Assamese text consistently uses the correct ৰ character rather than the Bengali র — since the test scans all leaf values (including nested objects and functions) to catch any stray wrong character. I'm also writing the pick, fixWhereLabel, and docTitle helper functions for language-aware label formatting.\n\nNow I'm moving to ai.ts: defining the AiResult and extraction return types with an error discriminator, then implementing extractDocument to validate the API key and image input, build an OpenAI-style chat completion request with a JSON-only system prompt and image content, parse and validate the response through RawExtraction and toExtractedDoc, retry once on failure, and fall back to an ai_failed error with logging for bad image responses.\n\nFor logging I'm structuring a safe info JSON event that includes model, prompt version, a hashed input, outcome, and attempt count, while never logging raw content, and adding a 30-second abort timeout. I'm confirming toExtractedDoc masks Aadhaar numbers and that every AI call routes through ai.ts with zod validation, skipping the server-only import since the package isn't installed and skipping an explainer as out of scope.\n\nNow I'm sketching the API routes: a POST /api/extract endpoint with zod-validated body and distinct status codes for success, ai_unavailable, ai_failed, and invalid input, and a POST /api/check endpoint that evaluates scheme eligibility given docs, answers, and an optional as-of date defaulting to today in the Asia/Kolkata timezone computed server-side.\n\nI'm deciding where to put the date helper — leaning toward a pure isoDateIn function in dates.ts using Intl.DateTimeFormat with an explicit timezone so it's testable, returning 404 for unknown scheme IDs, and checking whether cacheComponents has issues with exporting runtime nodejs on route handlers before confirming it's fine since only edge runtime is restricted. I'm also considering letting the UI import listSchemes directly from core since it's pure and client-safe, with actual evaluation still happening server-side.\n\nNow I'm planning the API test structure: putting tests alongside routes, using vi.mock on the ai module path so it resolves correctly regardless of import specifier, and sketching out extractDocument test cases covering missing API key, successful masked-aadhaar extraction, repeated bad JSON triggering ai_failed after two calls, and recovery when the first attempt fails but a retry succeeds.\n\nI'm also deciding to keep retry logic simple — just one retry on any failure regardless of error type — and adding log assertions to confirm no raw names leak while model, prompt version, and input hash are present. For dependency injection, I'm designing extractDocument to accept an optional fetch implementation and env object so tests don't rely on real environment variables, and I'm handling both raw base64 and data URL image formats with mime validation, plus client-side image resizing via canvas before upload to keep payloads small. I'm also noting that Next.js app route handlers don't impose the same body size limit as server actions, so request.json() should be fine for our purposes.\n\nNow I'm structuring the UI into a private `_components` folder, splitting the app into a main state machine component plus separate step components for scheme selection, document upload, confirmation, questions, and the final report, alongside shared UI primitives — keeping each file under size limits. I'm planning the state shape to track language, current step, scheme ID, extracted docs, answers, sample ID, and the report itself, with a language toggle that syncs to the document's lang attribute, and now I'm working out per-slot status tracking for the docs upload step.\n\nFor each document slot I'm adding buttons to take a photo via camera capture, manually type details, or remove the entry, plus sample buttons (A/B/C) that let users load a pre-filled scheme with demo documents and answers — filtered so only samples matching the currently selected scheme are shown, since some schemes have no samples available.\n\nPicking a sample loads its docs and answers and jumps straight to the confirm step, with a demo label persisting on later screens. For photo capture, I'm wiring the file picker to resize the image, send it to the extraction API with the document type as a hint, and either populate the document on success or fall back to a blank manual-entry form with a friendly error message if extraction fails or is unavailable. On the confirm step, I'm generating input fields for each document field so users can review and edit the extracted data.\n\nFor the Aadhaar field I'll show a masked hint like \"XXXX XXXX 4821\" and wire up the edit handler through setField, making sure input is validated to only accept numeric digits.\n\nI realize deriving the input value directly from the document would reset partial typing, so I need local draft state per field with a controlled input that commits on blur.\n\nStill writing field input logic...\n\nI'll go with draft plus blur commit, since clicking Next also triggers blur on both desktop and mobile. To avoid stale drafts when docs get replaced, I'll key ConfirmStep fields by a `docsVersion` counter that increments whenever docs are set from sample or AI.\n\nFor the questions step, I'll render yes/no buttons per scheme question with aria-pressed states and a counter for unanswered items, letting users continue regardless since unanswered maps to needs_verification. Submitting triggers the check API and generates the report, which shows eligibility and readiness banners, a compact \"check at the bank\" list of external items under the readiness banner, a \"fix before you apply\" section for action_required items, and a \"please verify\" section that also includes those external checks — accepting some duplication since the banner list is just names while \"please verify\" has full details.\n\nEach verification item will show its localized message, a \"Where\" label if applicable, a source link with verification date, and a \"provisional rule\" badge when relevant, collapsed into details for brevity. For benefits, I'll show the central share and state top-up separately, prompting local confirmation when top-up is null and only totaling when it's available, plus an override note. Supporting docs get a brief list with presence marks, and I'm adding a print cover sheet button that triggers printing with CSS to hide non-printable elements.\n\nI also need a print-only cover sheet showing scheme, date, verdicts, to-fix items, and masked applicant docs, using Tailwind's print: variant to hide nav buttons rather than custom CSS. The footer disclaimer should appear in both Assamese and English regardless of the active language, and a demo-mode banner should warn users when sample documents are being used, shown on each step after selection through to the printed report.\n\nFor fonts, I'm adding Noto Sans Bengali alongside Geist Sans so Bengali glyphs render properly while Latin text still uses Geist, with Bengali as the fallback in the font stack. The main page stays a server component rendering the client app, and I'm defining the server-side validation schema for the check route — scheme ID, extracted docs array, answers, and optional as-of date — with 400 errors on invalid JSON. I should also double check the framework's migration guide regarding runtime exports and route segment configs under the caching components feature.\n\nI've outlined the T2 plan: writing failing tests first, then building the core extraction/flow/report modules, server AI caller and routes, and the mobile UI flow, finishing with test/lint/build and a commit. Next I'll check the Cache Components notes on route config.\n\n"}}
