{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:30:09.183Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Node.js runtime should work fine with Cache Components, so I'll just verify that during the build. Now I'm setting up pure modules—starting with extraction.ts, which defines a lenient schema for raw extraction with a document type string and a fields record.\n\nFor the DOC_FIELDS mapping, I'm deciding to put it in a separate docFields.ts module since both extraction and flow need it, with flow re-exporting it so imports stay consistent. I'm defining which fields belong to each document type—aadhaar needs name, dob, and last four digits; bank passbook needs name, account number, and IFSC; land records need owner names while dropping fields like land area that aren't expected in the output.\n\nI'm also filling out the remaining doc types—ration card, death certificate, bpl proof, voter id, age proof, address proof, self declaration, income certificate, and other—keeping it simple with just name/dob fields, though I'm using \"husbandName\" as a stand-in field for death certificates. Now I'm thinking through how aadhaar numbers get parsed, specifically extracting the last four digits from a twelve-digit input.\n\nI'm figuring out how unknown docTypes should map to \"other\", and confidence mapping for raw AI fields—defaulting unlabelled confidence conservatively (below the confirmation threshold) so they always require user confirmation rather than being treated as verified.\n\nFor normalization logic, I'm working out how to clamp numeric confidence values into range, lower dob confidence when the date isn't a valid ISO format, and parse owner/name fields by splitting on delimiters, trimming, and dropping empties. I'm also deciding that only fields relevant to the detected docType get kept, and that aadhaarLast4 should be derived from whichever aadhaar-related field is present, extracting digits only.\n\nNow I'm checking the ifsc and accountNo handling (trim and stringify), then testing normalizeDob against a slash-formatted date to confirm it falls back to returning the trimmed input when it isn't a valid real date. Moving into flow.ts, I'm figuring out how docSlotsFor should determine which document slots are mandatory based on the scheme's required docs and name-check references.\n\nWriting more helper functions...\n\nI'm also thinking about the Aadhaar input UX: if setField clears the value once a partial 4-digit entry is below length, a controlled input could lose characters mid-typing. I should consider using a local draft state for the input with maxLength and numeric mode, syncing to the real field only once valid, rather than deriving directly from fieldText every keystroke.\n\nStill defining report view logic...\n\nNow I'm setting up the i18n module with language codes defaulting to Assamese, and a strings dictionary that supports both static text and functions for things like step counters that take parameters.\n\nI need to be careful with Assamese spelling — the test enforces using ৰ (U+09F0) and ৱ (U+09F1) rather than র, so document labels and fix-where labels need to use the correct characters when picking between languages.\n\nNext I'm sketching the AI document extraction function on the server, which takes an image and optional document type hint and returns either a successful parsed document or one of several error types like unavailable, failed, or invalid.\n\nI'm checking for the Cline API key and model from environment variables, failing gracefully if missing, then calling the chat completions endpoint with a system prompt plus the image as a data URL, requesting a JSON response at zero temperature, validating it against the raw extraction schema, retrying once on failure, and logging the call details including a hash of the input image.\n\nFor testing, I'll stub global fetch and environment variables rather than adding a fetch injection parameter, and also write a dedicated test file that exercises retry, fallback, and masking logic without real network calls. I'm also setting limits on image size and validating the mime type against allowed formats.\n\nFor the API routes, I'm defining a POST handler for extraction that validates the request body with zod, returns a 400 for bad input, and decides on an appropriate status code when the AI call fails versus succeeds.\n\nFor the check route, I'm planning a handler that accepts scheme ID, docs, and answers, defaults the evaluation date to today's date in the Asia/Kolkata timezone using an Intl.DateTimeFormat helper, returns 404 for unknown schemes and 400 with validation issues for invalid input, and validates the output against the Report schema.\n\nFor GET schemes, I'm deciding the client can import the core schemes registry directly since it's pure and zod-safe to bundle, using core for schemes/questions/samples/flow helpers while calling /api/check specifically for verdicts as the spec requires, and noting route tests will import the POST handler directly and mock the AI server module for the extract route.\n\nI'm placing test files outside the app directory, in src/server, to keep the app dir clean since Next only treats specific filenames as special there anyway. I'll keep route handlers thin, calling into server-side logic, and test the route POST functions directly; for the UI I'm planning a single client component to house the app.\n\nFor fonts, I'm adding Noto Sans Bengali via next/font/google alongside Geist, setting up a CSS variable fallback chain so Bengali text uses Noto while Latin text prefers Geist, since Geist doesn't cover Bengali glyphs.\n\nFor the language toggle, I'll use simple client state defaulting to Assamese, syncing the html lang attribute via an effect without persisting to localStorage to keep it minimal. For printing, I'm planning to let the report section itself double as the printable cover sheet, hiding non-report elements with a print-specific Tailwind variant, and including the scheme, date, verdicts, fix list, docs checklist, and masked Aadhaar in that view.\n\nFor the photo upload flow, each slot gets a file input that reads the image as a data URL via FileReader, strips the prefix, and posts it to the extraction endpoint with the mime type and doc hint -- though I should consider downscaling since phone photos can be quite large.\n\nWhen AI extraction fails, I'll show a fallback message prompting manual entry and add a blank doc for that slot so the form fields appear, plus a per-slot \"type details instead\" button. For the scheme step, I'm planning cards with a flagship badge for PM-KISAN, and sample buttons A/B/C tied to specific schemes that appear on the documents step.\n\nThe flow continues: the docs step lists each required slot with status and upload/type actions, the confirm step shows editable fields with low-confidence ones flagged amber for review, and the questions step presents yes/no toggles prefilled from samples while still allowing unanswered items.\n\nFor the overall shell, I'm adding a step indicator, back buttons, and a persistent \"Demo mode\" banner whenever sample data is in use, keeping that flag active until the user starts over or changes schemes. The report screen itself orders things as eligibility banner, readiness banner, then \"Fix before you apply\" and \"Please verify\" sections, with external checks surfaced as a clear \"check at the bank\" list.\n\nTo resolve the duplication question, I'll split \"Please verify\" into two sub-groups -- items to confirm internally versus items to confirm at the bank/office -- and also add a short note on the readiness banner itself flagging how many external confirmations are needed, linking down to the detailed list. Each verify item will show its message plus a \"Where\" label pointing to the fix location.\n\nFor the benefit card, I'm deciding to show the central share, state top-up (noting \"confirm locally\" when unknown), and total only if available, broken out by period, with any override note -- placed near the end after banners/fix/verify rather than interrupting that mandated order. Supporting docs like Orunodoi get a compact list with presence ticks, and the footer disclaimer should always show in both Assamese and English.\n\nThinking through the family-member-name question for PM-KISAN -- if it's unanswered it just stays unselected without triggering a verification flag, since it only matters for mismatch detection rather than being its own check. Now I'm starting to lay out the i18n string keys, beginning with the language toggle label.\n\nStill writing translation strings...\n\nI'm finishing out the dictionary structure with nav labels, error messages, and the aadhaar masking label, then deriving a shared `Dict` type from the English object so other locale objects must satisfy the same shape. I also need to settle whether \"needs_verification\" should be treated as distinct states for eligibility versus readiness or share one label.\n\nNow I'm drafting the Assamese translations carefully, making sure characters like ৰ and ৱ render correctly -- for example writing \"ধাপ ${n} / ${total}\" for step counters and working through terms like \"আঁচনি বাছক\" for scheme selection and \"মূল প্ৰদৰ্শন\" for flagship, double-checking the ৰ-ফলা conjunct is typed properly.\n\nStill writing translation strings...\n\nStill writing form field labels...\n\nStill writing translation strings...\n\nStill adding more translation keys...\n\nContinuing the error message, then adding cover sheet labels for applicant and submitted documents. I'm double-checking consistent use of ৰ instead of র throughout, including words like \"পৰৱৰ্তী\" and \"প্ৰিণ্ট\", before moving on to the English dictionary equivalent and Tailwind styling classes.\n\nPlanning the mobile-first layout with large touch-friendly buttons, plus accessibility features like live status regions and focus management when steps change. I'm organizing the component structure into separate files under a private Next.js folder—main app state machine, individual step components, a report view, shared UI elements, and an image downscaling utility—and will write each file separately given editor length limits.\n\nI'll now implement the plan: pure core logic (doc fields, extraction, flow, report view), i18n strings, server routes with AI calling, and the UI flow, then verify with tests/typecheck/lint/build before committing.\n\n"}}
