{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:31:41.305Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I technically wrote tests and implementations together rather than strictly test-first, so I'll just note that discrepancy. Now moving to i18n.ts, I need to make sure `docTitle` returns the capitalized \"Bank passbook\" string, and that the Assamese translation avoids using the character র (U+09B0).\n\nI need to use ৰ instead of র, and ৱ where appropriate in Assamese words, though the test only checks for the র character specifically. I'm sketching out the full set of UI strings needed—language toggle, step labels, navigation buttons, scheme picker text, demo mode messaging, sample document labels, and document upload flow strings—designing these as a Lang-keyed record where some values are functions like stepOf(n, total) that need to be loosely typed enough to accept the test's call signatures.\n\nNow I'm continuing to flesh out the remaining string categories: confirmation screen labels (field names, confidence warnings, hints for owner names and Aadhaar digits), the questions section, and the full report section covering eligibility verdicts, benefit breakdowns, source citations, and disclaimer text.\n\nFor the footer, I'm reusing the existing disclaimer label since it's close enough to the task's requested wording, and noting the report already carries bilingual disclaimer fields. For printing, I'm planning to keep things simple by using the browser's print function with dedicated print styles that hide navigation buttons and show a compact cover sheet block only when printing.\n\nNow I'm drafting Assamese vocabulary for the UI labels — terms for step indicators, back/next/start-over navigation, scheme selection, and flagship labeling.\n\nContinuing through demo mode text, document upload options (photo capture vs manual entry), loading states, and the fallback message explaining when automatic reading isn't available and users must enter details manually.\n\nWorking through more label strings: added/not-added states, remove button, required/optional field markers, confirmation screen title and low-confidence warning text, the confirm button label, and field labels like name and date of birth.\n\nContinuing with more field labels for Aadhaar digits, bank account number, IFSC code, land owner's name, husband's name, and land area in hectares, plus question/answer labels (yes/no) and report section headers for eligibility and readiness.\n\nNow I'm adding status labels like likely eligible, not eligible, needs verification, ready for review, and action required, along with titles for fixing issues before application, verification prompts, and an external note about confirming at the bank or office.\n\nI'm also adding fields for source, verification date, provisional rules, and benefit breakdowns separating central and state contributions, with local confirmation notes for state top-ups, plus totals shown per month, per year, and in instalments.\n\nThen I'm adding labels for printing a cover sheet, a checking-in-progress status, and a generic error message, along with a helper to pick between English and Assamese text and a function to capitalize document titles.\n\nNow I'm moving to the AI extraction logic, defining a function that takes an image and an optional document type hint and returns either the extracted document data or an error.\n\nI'm calling the Cline API with an OpenAI-compatible chat completions format, requesting JSON output and passing the image as a data URL, while reading the API key and model from environment variables and failing gracefully if they're missing. I'm validating the response against a schema, converting it into the extracted document shape, retrying once on failure, and logging the model, prompt version, and a hash of the input image without ever logging raw data. I'm also handling image size limits and mime type detection, and considering whether to make the fetch call injectable for easier testing.\n\nFor testing, I'm planning to mock the global fetch so I can verify behaviors like missing API key returning ai_unavailable, the retry-then-fallback logic, and the success path masking Aadhaar numbers, all without hitting the network. I'm confirming Next.js loads environment variables automatically for dev and build, then sketching out the extract API route: a POST handler that validates an incoming JSON payload with the base64 image and optional doc type using zod, calls the extraction function, and returns the extracted document or an appropriate error status.\n\nFor error handling I'm deciding on status codes — 503 for unavailable, 502 for failed extraction, 400 for bad input — with the UI reading the error field from the response. I'm also designing the check route that accepts scheme ID, version, documents, answers, and an optional as-of date defaulting to today in Asia/Kolkata time, validating input with zod, returning 404 for unknown schemes, and trusting that the evaluation output already conforms to the Report schema since the core logic is pure.\n\nFor scheme and sample data on the client side, I'm thinking the page should be a server component that computes the scheme list, questions, and document slots using the in-memory registry, then passes them as serializable props to a client component — this keeps things forward-compatible for when scheme versions move into SQLite later.\n\nImporting questionsFor pulls in the whole rule-pack registry and some evaluate helpers client-side, which adds some bundle weight but it's all pure code, so I think it's acceptable rather than worth re-architecting around.\n\nFor API tests, I'm considering testing route handlers directly by calling POST with a constructed Request and mocking the AI module, and I'm weighing whether to colocate test files under the app directory or keep them under src/server importing from the route module instead.\n\nI'll add structured logging in ai.ts for each call with model, prompt version, hash, attempt, and outcome, plus client-side image resizing before upload to keep payloads small on mobile. Now I'm planning the UI structure with a private components folder containing the flow state machine and separate step components for scheme selection, document upload, and confirmation.\n\nFor state, I'll track language, current step, selected scheme, extracted documents, answers, sample ID, and report data. The scheme step needs cards with flagship badges and sample A/B/C buttons that pre-populate scheme, docs, and answers to jump ahead in the flow.\n\nThe docs step shows one card per required slot with status badges and two actions: \"Take photo\" using a camera-capturing file input, or \"Type details\" which adds a blank document for manual entry, with samples filtered by the selected scheme (A/B for PM-KISAN, C for widow pension, none for Orunodoi).\n\nAdded docs show a summary with a remove option, and if AI extraction fails after a photo, it falls back to a blank doc for manual entry. The confirm step lets users edit fields, flags low-confidence ones in yellow needing review, and stores only the last four digits of Aadhaar even if a user types the full number.\n\nFor DOB, I'm leaning toward a plain text input with a DD/MM/YYYY placeholder rather than a native date picker, since normalizeDob can convert that format but a date input would just show blank on unparsable AI output — though I need to be careful not to normalize incomplete input while the user is still typing.\n\nTo avoid jarring mid-typing conversions, I'll give each field local draft state and commit/normalize only on blur, making sure blur fires before any \"Next\" click so nothing gets lost; fields will need keys tied to doc/sample so switching documents resets the draft properly.\n\nFor the questions step I'll use large Yes/No toggle buttons with aria-pressed, showing answered count, and a \"See result\" button that posts to the check API before moving to the report step with banners, benefits, print option, and a start-over control. The language toggle also needs to update document.documentElement.lang via an effect, which is safe since it doesn't involve setState. For fonts, I'll switch to Noto Sans Bengali from next/font/google (covering both Bengali and Latin subsets) in place of Geist sans, since network access for Google fonts worked in the previous build.\n\nI'll stack Geist and Noto Sans Bengali as the sans font variable so Latin uses Geist and Bengali falls back to Noto automatically. I also need to fix the globals.css body style, which currently hardcodes Arial and overrides the font variable—I'll update it to reference the combined font stack with system-ui and sans-serif fallbacks. For theming, I'm considering just forcing a light color scheme to keep things simple rather than dealing with dark mode variants.\n\nFor print styles, I'll use Tailwind's print variant to hide interactive elements and show a dedicated cover sheet only when printing. Now I'm starting the server-side page component, importing scheme listing, question, and flow utilities along with sample data.\n\nWriting the Home component...\n\nI'm checking the ordering of schemes—pm-kisan should come first as the flagship—and confirming the insertion order already matches that. I'm also tracing whether the client-side flow module pulls scheme evaluation logic into the client bundle unnecessarily, since that could bloat things if not isolated properly.\n\nThe registry's module-level side effects mean tree-shaking won't drop the unused imports, but the bundle cost from the rule packs is small enough to accept. I'm deciding to refactor so relevantAnswers takes a list of questions directly rather than depending on the scheme registry.\n\nI also notice LOW_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD pulls in evaluate plus its dependencies, so I'm moving that constant into a tiny thresholds module and re-exporting it from evaluate to keep the API unchanged. Then I need to update the flow test to pass the questions list into relevantAnswers.\n\nChecking the remaining imports, reportView only needs types, extraction pulls in zod/dates/docFields/types, and flow pulls docFields/thresholds/extraction/types -- all looks clean. Now I'm writing i18n.ts, defining the English strings object and typing the Assamese translations to match its shape exactly, including the numeric formatter functions, making sure to get the Assamese digit handling right.\n\nI've finished the core pure modules. Now I'll extract the confidence threshold into a small module re-exported from `evaluate.ts` to keep the client bundle free of the rule engine, and update `relevantAnswers` to accept a question list.\n\n"}}
