{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:34:16.722Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now I'm designing the i18n structure, making sure Assamese uses the correct ৰ and ৱ characters rather than Bengali equivalents, and planning out the UI flow steps (scheme, docs, confirm, questions, report) along with the string keys needed like appName, tagline, and langToggle.\n\nI'm now drafting the full set of UI strings for each step: language toggle labels, step navigation text, scheme selection prompts, sample/demo mode labels, document upload instructions (photo capture, typing, reading states, error messages), and the confirmation screen fields covering name, dates, account details, and related hints.\n\nI'm also writing out the questions and eligibility report strings — verdict labels, fix/verify/ok sections, benefit breakdowns like central share, state top-up, totals, and disclaimers — plus the print-friendly cover sheet text showing the applicant's prepared-on date.\n\nFor printing, I'll just use window.print() with print styles hiding the buttons — straightforward.\n\nNow I'm drafting the Assamese translations, being careful to use ৰ (the correct Assamese ra character) instead of the Bengali র everywhere, since that's a common and risky mistake — things like \"scheme\" as \"আঁচনি\" and step labels will need verification against the test.\n\nContinuing to fill out the Assamese strings for navigation (back/next/start over), sample document labels, demo mode text, and the document upload section labels like required/optional, take photo, and type it yourself.\n\nAdding more translation strings...\n\nStill adding more field labels and hints for account details, owner names, and a short questions section with yes/no options...\n\nStill adding verdict labels and section strings for the eligibility checker translations...\n\nStill adding more translation entries...\n\nI'm also double-checking the use of ৱ versus ব across words like \"ব্যৱহাৰ\" and \"স্বয়ংক্ৰিয়ভাৱে\" to make sure the spelling conventions stay consistent throughout.\n\nNow I'm defining the i18n type structure with a `Strings` type derived from the English object, setting up helper functions like docTitle, fixWhereLabel, and pick for language-specific labels, and separating eligibility and readiness verdict maps since shared keys like \"needs_verification\" need different wording in each context.\n\nMoving to server-side ai.ts, I'm designing it as the sole model caller with structured JSON output validated via zod, one retry, a safe fallback, and logging of model, prompt version, and input hash. Missing API key returns an \"ai_unavailable\" error, Aadhaar masking happens inside ai.ts through toExtractedDoc, and raw images are never stored. I'm defining the error types and the extractDocument function signature that takes an image, optional document type hint, and dependencies for fetch/env/logging, with tests mocking ai.ts for API-level testing.\n\nFor ai.ts's own unit tests, I'll inject a fake fetch to avoid real network calls. Configuration pulls the API key and model from environment variables with a constant base URL, and requests go to the chat completions endpoint with a system prompt plus image content, zero temperature. I'm second-guessing whether to request a JSON response format since not all providers support it reliably through Cline — safer to just instruct the model to return JSON only and parse the response content, stripping any markdown code fences if present.\n\nFor image handling, I'll accept either a data URL or raw base64, detecting MIME type from the data URL prefix with a default of image/jpeg, enforce a size limit around 6MB, and validate the base64 characters. I'm adding a 30-second timeout via AbortSignal, with a single retry on any failure other than a missing API key. For logging, I'm structuring JSON log lines capturing the task, model, prompt version, an input hash, attempt number, outcome, and duration in milliseconds.\n\nThe input hash will be a truncated SHA-256 of the image base64 plus hint, never logging raw content. I'm skipping the \"server-only\" package since it's not installed and likely won't resolve under test environments, so ai.ts will just read env vars at call time rather than module load, keeping secrets out of logs. Now I'm sketching the API route: a POST /api/extract endpoint that validates the body with zod, calls extractDocument, and returns success or appropriate error codes depending on whether the AI is unavailable, failed, or the input was invalid.\n\nFor payload size, since route handlers don't impose an explicit body limit, I'm planning to have the client downscale images via canvas to a max 1600px JPEG at 0.85 quality before upload, keeping payloads around 300KB which is friendlier for mobile. I'm also designing POST /api/check to take scheme, docs, and answers and return a Report, defaulting asOfDate to today in Asia/Kolkata computed server-side, with 404 for unknown schemes and 400 for invalid input. For the scheme list and questions needed by the UI, I'm considering whether the client component can just import directly from the pure core/schemes module since it's lightweight enough to bundle entirely.\n\nActually, the cleaner approach is having page.tsx as a server component that computes scheme summaries and sample data, then passes everything as props to the client component, avoiding an extra API endpoint entirely. Since this page has no dynamic data, it stays static under cacheComponents, while the POST route for checking remains uncached and returns asOfDate in its report for display purposes. I'm also noting that runtime config should still work fine with cacheComponents even though dynamic/revalidate/fetchCache configs are disallowed, and that I can test route handlers directly by importing the POST function in tests.\n\nFor organizing tests, I'm leaning toward putting shared logic in a handlers module with pure functions that take parsed request bodies, keeping the route files thin wrappers, so tests can target either the handler functions directly or the route POST exports with mocked AI calls.\n\nI'll keep the route logic inline rather than extracting a separate service file, and add a small helper that formats today's date in Asia/Kolkata timezone using Intl.DateTimeFormat for the asOfDate field. For the UI, I'm planning a single client component that manages the step-by-step flow of the app.\n\nI'll organize the step views into private component files under `_components/` — things like App, SchemeStep, DocsStep, ConfirmStep, QuestionsStep, ReportView, plus a shared ui.tsx for reusable Button/Card/Banner elements. I also need to define a SchemeSummary type covering id, version, names, flagship flag, slots, questions, and benefit view, along with a builder function to convert a scheme into that summary shape.\n\nFor scheme cards I'll keep things minimal — just name plus flagship badge, skipping benefit details on the card itself. Sample documents (\"Use sample A/B/C\") should be tied to the selected scheme and shown on the docs step rather than cluttering the home screen, though schemes without samples like Orunodoi won't show that option.\n\nUsing a sample should populate docs and answers in demo mode, carrying the user through confirm (with nothing flagged) and prefilled questions to the report, giving a quick but complete walkthrough. A demo-mode banner should persist across steps once activated, staying true until the user starts over or switches schemes, even if they edit the sample's docs afterward.\n\nEach doc slot shows a card with title, required/optional badge, and status, offering buttons to take or upload a photo, type the info manually, or remove the doc -- with \"type it in\" adding a blank doc that gets filled in on the confirm step, and failed AI extraction falling back to this same manual blank-doc path automatically. I'm weighing whether to detect AI availability ahead of time via an environment check, but that risks getting baked in statically at build time and going stale, so it's probably better to just let failures be handled reactively rather than pre-checking availability.\n\nOnce the first extraction fails with an \"AI unavailable\" response, I'll set a flag to hide the photo buttons for the rest of the session and show a note that automatic reading is off, directing users to type instead. For photo capture itself, I'm processing the image entirely client-side -- downscaling it via canvas before converting to a compressed JPEG data URL and sending it to the extraction endpoint, discarding the image data afterward rather than storing it. The confirm step then renders each document as a card with its fields, flagging low-confidence fields with a yellow border and a \"this is correct\" confirmation button.\n\nI realize that committing trimmed values on every keystroke would break typing (trimming would eat trailing spaces mid-input), so instead each field needs local uncommitted text state that only syncs to the real field value on blur. But I also need to watch out for the mobile quirk where tapping \"Next\" blurs the input first, so the click handler's closure might not see the just-committed value in time.\n\nLuckily React flushes discrete event updates synchronously before the click fires, so the new value should be available by the time \"Next\" runs. For the Aadhaar field, I'll just constrain it to numeric input with a max length of 4 digits, with a hint showing it only needs the last 4 digits.\n\nFor the Questions step, I'm planning Yes/No toggle buttons for each question with aria-pressed state, feeding into a \"See result\" button that posts the scheme, docs, and relevant answers to the check endpoint. The report screen then shows a demo label if applicable, eligibility and readiness banners with short explanations, a visible \"check at the bank/office\" list of external verification items under the readiness banner, and then organized Fix/Verify/OK sections with the OK items collapsed by default.\n\nExternal checks need to appear both under the banner (as short labels pulled from the scheme's externalChecks metadata) and within the Verify section with fuller detail — each item showing the message, where to fix it if applicable, a source link, verification date, and a provisional badge when relevant.\n\nFor the benefit card, I'm laying out central share, state top-up, total amount, period, and instalments, with the Orunodoi example showing zero central share plus a full state top-up totaling ₹1,250/month, alongside provenance and override notes. I also need supporting docs for Orunodoi, the rule version and asOf date, and a print cover sheet feature that hides non-printable elements and shows a minimal print-only header with title, scheme, date, verdicts, and applicant info.\n\nThe cover sheet should also list attached documents with checkmarks, respecting the current language setting, and the footer must always show the bilingual disclaimer in both Assamese and English. I'm adding a \"Start over\" button and a language toggle in the header that switches between Assamese and English and updates the document's lang attribute.\n\nFor typography, I'll bring in Noto Sans Bengali via next/font/google alongside Geist, wiring it as the primary sans font with Geist as fallback, and replace the hardcoded Arial in globals.css with this font variable. Then I'll set up the app's core state with step, language, scheme ID, documents, and answers tracked via useState.\n\nThe scheme registry can stay in-memory and build statically since page.tsx just calls listSchemes() as a pure server component — runtime registration can come later. I'll skip localStorage persistence for language for now, and watch out for the React Compiler lint rules in Next 16 that flag setState calls inside effects, preferring a key-based remount approach for resetting FieldInput's local state instead of using an effect.\n\nFor the key, I can derive it from the doc type, field name, and the committed field text so that typing locally doesn't trigger a remount, but committing on blur (which changes the underlying doc) does — this way focus isn't disrupted since blur already occurred before the remount happens.\n\nFor language toggling, I'll keep the html lang attribute static in the layout but update `document.documentElement.lang` as a side effect in a useEffect when the toggle changes.\n\nI'm also deciding to keep the scheme summary builder server-side in its own module that imports the schemes registry, so only the server page touches it and the client bundle stays lean with just type imports.\n\nThe scheme summary should include questions with their provenance so provisional-rule questions can get a badge, flagship status flagged by id for pm-kisan with flagship-first ordering, and per-scheme sample data passed down as serializable props. I'm also setting up the fix-before-apply labels and external links with target _blank and rel noopener, plus a small i18n helper that picks between English and Assamese strings based on the current language.\n\nNow I'm thinking through tone styling conventions — green for success, red for errors, amber for warnings, zinc for neutral — then writing out the Assamese translations carefully, making sure to consistently use ৰ rather than the alternate র form throughout all the terms.\n\n"}}
