{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:37:15.517Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now that tests pass, I'm designing the server-side ai.ts module as the sole model caller, plus testable API route handlers. I'm planning a config loader that pulls API key, model, and base URL from environment, and an extraction function that takes an image and optional document type hint, returning either a successful document result or a typed error like unavailable, failed, or invalid image.\n\nThe extraction logic will call an OpenAI-compatible chat completions endpoint with the image as a data URL, request JSON-formatted output, validate it against a schema, and convert it to the final extracted document shape—with one retry on failure and careful logging that avoids leaking raw content, only logging the model, prompt version, and a truncated hash of the input. I'm making the fetch call and dependencies injectable so I can unit-test this without hitting the network, matching the mocking approach the API tests will use.\n\nI'm also setting constraints like a size limit on base64 images, mime-type detection from data URL prefixes, and a 30-second timeout via AbortSignal, while deciding to keep route handlers importable directly in route files so tests can call POST on a constructed Request and mock the ai module cleanly.\n\nFor the check endpoint, I'm validating the request body with scheme id, version, extracted docs, answers, and an optional as-of date defaulting to today in Asia/Kolkata—computed inside the route handler since that's fine for POST requests even with cacheComponents. For scheme metadata on the client, I'm leaning toward having the server component pass scheme summaries as props rather than importing the core schemes module directly into the client bundle, to avoid divergence between server and client registries as more scheme versions get added later; I'll shape a SchemeSummary view containing id, version, localized names, document slots, questions with provenance status, and benefit details.\n\nI'm placing a summarizeScheme function in a separate schemeSummary module that pulls in questionsFor from the registry, keeping it server-only so the client only imports the type, which gets erased at build time. For sample data, since zod and extraction types already end up in the client bundle via the flow module anyway, I'm considering just passing samples as props from the page component too, to keep the client as light as possible.\n\nI'm laying out the component structure: a server page rendering a client Flow component that manages state across scheme, docs, confirm, questions, and report steps, with separate step components for each stage plus a report view with a print-friendly cover sheet. I'm also planning language toggling to update the document's lang attribute, and adding sample data buttons (A/B/C) on the docs step for quick testing.\n\nI should keep the demo label active as long as sampleId is set, resetting only on start-over or scheme change. For photo capture, I'll downscale large phone photos via canvas to a reasonable JPEG size before sending to the extract API, then handle errors by falling back to manual entry with an appropriate message, including a specific case for AI unavailability.\n\nRather than baking AI availability into a server component at build time via environment variables (which would be stale if config changes later), I'll track aiAvailable reactively on the client after the first failure, keeping photo buttons visible alongside a \"type it in\" fallback. For the document slot cards, each shows its status with photo/type-it-in buttons and a remove option once added, where \"type it in\" creates a blank document entry.\n\nI'm weighing whether manual entry should edit fields inline right in the docs step versus deferring all editing to the confirm step. Duplicating a field editor component across both steps feels redundant, so I'm leaning toward: docs step just marks the document as added, and the confirm step is where all fields, including blank ones from manual entry, get typed in and low-confidence extractions get highlighted.\n\nI'm also realizing the confirm step inputs need careful handling since parsing on every keystroke (like validating a 4-digit Aadhaar number) would reject partial input while typing, so I need to keep raw draft text per field and only commit the parsed value on blur.\n\nFor low-confidence fields, I'll add an amber highlight with a \"please check\" message and a confirm button, and editing the field counts as confirmation too — but I won't block the user from proceeding while fields are unconfirmed, just flag them as needing verification with a visible count.\n\nFor the questions step, I'm planning yes/no toggles with progress tracking and a button to submit for checking. On the report, I'm working through how to display the readiness banner's list of external checks — since each check only has a long descriptive message rather than a short label, I'll need to either show the full message compactly or derive something shorter from the fix location and message text.\n\nActually, the summary already includes short labels for external checks (in both languages), so I can use those in the banner while saving the full verification message for a separate \"please verify\" section. I'm also outlining the benefit card layout showing the share, top-up status, totals, and period details, then organizing the report into fix/verify/OK sections with sources, dates, provisional badges, supporting documents, and a bilingual footer disclaimer.\n\nFor printing, I'll add a \"Print cover sheet\" button using window.print() with print-specific Tailwind classes to hide the app UI and reveal a dedicated cover sheet containing the title, scheme info, masked Aadhaar, document list, verdicts, and disclaimer. I'm also setting up Noto Sans Bengali via next/font/google for proper font rendering.\n\nI'll order Geist first then Noto Sans Bengali, letting the browser fall back per-glyph so Bengali/Assamese script renders consistently from Noto while Latin stays Geist. For dark mode, I'll keep things simple by sticking to explicit light colors like white cards rather than relying on the existing dark media query, since that could clash with my component backgrounds.\n\nFor hooks, I'll keep the language-sync effect side-effect-free by just setting `document.documentElement.lang` without any state updates, avoiding newer lint rules around setState-in-effect. On the client, I'll POST to the check API with the relevant payload as JSON, and since the date isn't sent from the client, the server will resolve \"today\" using Asia/Kolkata timezone.\n\nI'll write a small helper using Intl.DateTimeFormat with the en-CA locale and Kolkata timezone to produce a YYYY-MM-DD string, placing it in src/server/today.ts, and make sure to avoid calling `new Date()` during render in prerendered client components to stay compatible with cacheComponents. I'm also double-checking that synchronous calls like listSchemes() in page.tsx are fine under cacheComponents, and starting on the ai.ts module, skipping the \"server-only\" import since that package isn't installed.\n\nNow I'm defining the AI extraction types and error constants, settling on a discriminated result shape where failures return an `{ error: \"ai_unavailable\" }`-style object per the spec, matching errors like ai_unavailable, ai_failed, and invalid_image to a consistent error shape across the API.\n\nI'm sketching out the `extractDocument` function signature with injectable dependencies (fetch, env, logging, timeout) and planning its validation steps: first checking config for missing API key/model and logging+returning an unavailable error, then parsing and validating the base64 image against size and format constraints.\n\nI'm also mapping out the retry logic—hashing the image input, attempting the API call up to twice, parsing the JSON response (stripping code fences), validating it against a schema, and converting it to the final document shape, with a simple fallback error on failure and structured logging throughout.\n\nWithin ai.ts, I'm ensuring Aadhaar numbers are masked to only the last 4 digits before they ever leave the function, and I'm writing the system prompt instructing the model to extract specific fields (name, dob, Aadhaar last 4, account number, IFSC, owner names, husband's name) with per-field confidence scores, while explicitly telling it never to guess or return full Aadhaar numbers.\n\nFor the request itself, I'm structuring the message payload with a text hint about the expected doc type plus the base64-encoded image, setting temperature to 0 for consistency, and deciding to skip the `response_format` JSON mode since I'm unsure the API supports it—better to parse the JSON out of the raw text response manually. I'm also working out how to safely extract the message content from the response structure, since it could come back as either a plain string or an array of parts.\n\nFor the actual API route, I'm validating the incoming request body with a schema limiting base64 size and an optional docType, returning a 400 for malformed requests, then mapping different failure modes (unavailable AI, failed extraction, invalid image) to distinct status codes while keeping the client's JSON parsing consistent regardless of status.\n\nFor /api/check, I'm validating scheme id/version, document array, answers, and an optional as-of date, looking up the scheme and returning 404 if unknown, then running the evaluation against today's date in India if none is given. I'm planning test files for both routes to cover these cases.\n\nI'm deciding where to place the test files, favoring a single colocated file in the api directory, and confirming vitest's alias resolution will let me mock the AI server module correctly for both the route tests and the AI module tests with an injected fetch.\n\nFor the AI module tests, I'm covering missing key failures, successful parsing with masked Aadhaar data, retry-then-fail behavior, and ensuring logs never leak names or Aadhaar numbers. For the API tests, I'm planning coverage across sample documents, manual entry fallback, error codes for unknown schemes and bad bodies, and the specific case where AI is unavailable but manual entry still completes the flow successfully.\n\nNow I'm moving on to writing ai.ts.\n\n"}}
