{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:40:20.171Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now I'm designing the API routes layer, keeping handlers thin and testable in a separate file. I'm planning the extract endpoint to accept an image and optional doc type, returning the result as 200 with either the parsed document or a graceful error payload so the UI can handle AI failures smoothly.\n\nFor status codes, I'm settling on 200 for success, 400 for invalid image, 503 when AI is unavailable, and 502 for AI failures, with the UI parsing JSON regardless of status. I'm also planning the check endpoint, defaulting asOfDate to today in Asia/Kolkata computed server-side, validating input with zod, and returning 404 for unknown schemes, while considering how the scheme registry data gets passed to client components given caching constraints.\n\nSince zod and runtime types already leak into the client bundle through the flow module, I decide it's fine to pass samples through normally too, but for per-scheme questions I'll build a server-side SchemeView object instead of importing the full registry client-side, keeping the client bundle lean.\n\nI'll put the pure schemeCatalog() builder in core/catalog.ts since it's framework-agnostic, with the report screen looking up scheme names from that catalog client-side using the schemeId.\n\nFor the client itself, I'm planning a single KaagazApp component managing the step state machine (scheme, docs, confirm, questions, report), broken into private step components under an underscore folder, including photo upload with canvas-based downscaling for the docs step.\n\nFor language, I'll keep lang in component state and sync it to document.documentElement.lang via useEffect rather than persisting to localStorage, keeping things minimal. For the sample/demo flow, selecting a sample sets scheme, docs, and prefilled answers with demo=true and jumps straight to the confirm step, so users see the confirmation screen with high-confidence sample docs before moving through questions to the report — a quick three-click path. I'll show samples filtered by the selected scheme within the docs step itself, rather than adding a separate demo row on the scheme step.\n\nI'll keep a persistent demo banner across steps and on the report while demo mode is active, clearing it only on start over or scheme change rather than trying to detect mixed real/fake document edits, which would add unnecessary complexity. Now I'm setting up the file input for photo uploads, restricting it to image types.\n\nChecking env at build time vs runtime is messy since a key added later wouldn't be picked up without a rebuild, so I'll skip that complexity and just let the UI attempt the photo flow — if it returns ai_unavailable, show the message and fall back to manual entry, matching what NEXT.md describes. For manual entry, each slot card gets a \"Type it in\" button to trigger the fallback form.\n\nInstead, I'd rather keep field editing centralized in the Confirm step — each slot's \"Type it in\" button marks a blank doc as added, and editable fields for every added doc appear together in Confirm, where missing fields simply flag as needs_verification.\n\nFor the Questions step, I'm listing scheme questions with toggled Yes/No buttons and provisional badges, confirming that the familyOwnerQuestion doesn't factor into mismatchCheck or questionChecks, so leaving it unanswered is fine per the sample data.\n\nFor the report step, I'll trigger the API check from the \"See the result\" button's click handler rather than an effect, since Next 16's eslint rules now forbid synchronous setState inside effects — the handler will fetch and store the report while transitioning to the report step. The language-setting effect is safe since it only touches the DOM, not React state.\n\nWorking through the report layout, I'm sequencing demo label, scheme metadata, eligibility banner, readiness banner with a short external-checks list, then deciding where benefit amount fits relative to the fix/verify/OK sections — I'll place it after those sections but before supporting docs since the spec's ordering doesn't explicitly cover it.\n\nFor the fix/verify sections, I'm planning cards with message, location, source link, verification date, and a provisional badge, falling back to \"Nothing to fix\" when empty, with OK items collapsed in a details element and both-language footer disclaimers always visible.\n\nFor printing, I'm adding a print-only cover sheet showing applicant name, scheme, date, masked Aadhaar, verdicts, and disclaimer, hidden from the main screen view and shown only via print styles; I also need to confirm the build can fetch Google Fonts for Noto Sans Bengali alongside the existing Geist font, keeping Geist for Latin text and adding the Bengali variable font for body text.\n\nActually I'm simplifying by dropping Geist entirely and using Noto Sans Bengali for everything since it covers Latin too, overriding the Arial default in globals.css, and forcing a light-only color scheme to avoid dark mode conflicts with my light-card design.\n\nNow I'm sketching a catalog module that defines a SchemeView type and a function to convert a raw SchemeRule into that display-friendly shape, bundling fields like name, flagship status, document slots, questions, and external references.\n\nFlagship status is just a check for pm-kisan, and scheme order follows registration order. I'm also planning to attach sample IDs per scheme by pulling from the SAMPLES data, and to show the benefit amount as a small label like \"₹6,000 / year\" on each scheme card.\n\nFor the card benefit line, I'll skip full date-aware computation and just display the base amount simply: central + state top-up where relevant, like \"₹1,250 per month\" for Orunodoi or \"₹300 per month + state top-up\" for the widow scheme, computing it inline with formatInr rather than building a separate helper.\n\nNow I'm sketching the API handler module with Zod schemas for extract and check requests — defining the request shapes for image extraction and scheme eligibility checks, including doc arrays, answers, and optional as-of dates, then building out the corresponding handler functions.\n\nFor testing, I'm planning to mock the ai module at its resolved path so both relative and alias imports in api.ts pick up the mock, then write route-level tests importing the Next.js route handlers directly, placing test files alongside the server code to match the vitest include pattern.\n\nI'll avoid any next/server imports in the route files, using plain Response.json instead so they're testable in a plain node environment. For today's date in India I'm using Intl.DateTimeFormat with Asia/Kolkata timezone, and confirming the zod schema strictly validates extracted docs so no full Aadhaar number can leak through, only the last 4 digits.\n\nTime to write the catalog, the API routes, and the tests now.\n\n"}}
