{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:42:23.363Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"It's odd that the handler itself returns 502 properly, yet vitest still flags the thrown error as a test failure, even though the error should be caught internally. I wonder if this is some Vitest 5 quirk around mock rejections and resets being tracked as unhandled despite the catch block handling it.\n\nWait, I think I found it — the `beforeEach(() => extractMock.mockReset())` arrow function returns the mock itself, which is a function. Vitest treats a returned function from `beforeEach` as a teardown callback, so it calls that mock as cleanup after the test, which throws \"boom\". The fix is just wrapping it in braces so nothing is returned.\n\nFound the bug: `beforeEach(() => extractMock.mockReset())` returns the mock, which Vitest treats as a teardown function, causing it to re-invoke the mock (and throw \"boom\") after the test. I'll fix it by giving the hook a block body.\n\n"}}
