{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:43:38.647Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"The teardown issue was caused by beforeEach returning the mock function itself, which got interpreted as cleanup logic. That's fixed now, all 148 tests pass. Moving to the UI, I'm planning a server component page that builds the catalog from listSchemes and passes it along with sample data to a client component, keeping things static-friendly, though I need to check how importing core modules into the client component will behave.\n\nSamples should be passed as plain serialisable props rather than imported directly in the client, since importing would pull in zod and other core types unnecessarily. I'm organizing client components into a private `_components` folder: a top-level `KaagazApp` managing the state machine for language, step, scheme, documents, answers, and report, plus separate step components for scheme selection, document upload, confirmation, questions, and report viewing, along with a client-side image downscaling helper using canvas. Bundling zod client-side via `@/core/flow` is acceptable for now, and `@/i18n` and `@/core/reportView` are pure enough to import safely.\n\nFor language handling, I'm defaulting to Assamese and toggling both state and the document's lang attribute, keeping the server-rendered html lang in sync. For fonts, I'm using Noto Sans Bengali through next/font/google with Bengali and Latin subsets, confirming network access works since Geist already built successfully earlier.\n\nI'm setting the body font stack so Geist handles Latin glyphs first and Noto Sans Bengali covers Bengali script as a fallback, relying on per-glyph font fallback in the browser. For printing, I'm planning a cover sheet triggered by window.print(), using Tailwind's print: variants to hide interactive UI and show a dedicated print-only section. I'm now sketching the report layout order: a demo mode indicator when a sample is active, eligibility and readiness banners with tone-based coloring, a list of bank-side checks, and then the benefit amount section.\n\nContinuing the report structure: after the benefit breakdown I'm outlining the \"fix before you apply\" and \"please verify\" lists, a collapsed section for passing checks, supporting documents, print/start-over actions, and a bilingual disclaimer footer shown in both Assamese and English. For each check item, I want to show the message, an optional \"where to fix\" label, a source link with verification date, and a provisional badge when the provenance status calls for it — plus I'm thinking through how to handle the check step when the AI extraction endpoint returns unavailable.\n\nI'm deciding to discover AI availability lazily at the first photo attempt rather than baking a config flag in at build time, since reading environment variables in a server component during prerender with cached components wouldn't reflect runtime state. For the Docs step, I'm laying out each document slot as a card showing its title, a required badge, its current status, and buttons to either capture/upload a photo or manually type in the details — with manual entry simply adding a blank document marked as added, deferring actual field editing to the Confirm step.\n\nThen on Confirm, all added docs get editable fields with a remove option, and photo uploads show a spinner before upserting the result (or falling back to a blank doc with an error message on failure). I'm also considering whether to surface sample documents on the Docs step itself, filtered by the active scheme — showing samples A/B for pm-kisan, C for widow, and none for Orunodoi.\n\nPicking a sample sets both the docs and answers from the sample data and tags the session with a demo label, which persists even if the user edits fields afterward; starting over clears it. On Confirm, each document shows its title plus fields for its type, with ownerNames using a textarea and flagged fields getting an amber highlight with a check prompt.\n\nI'm also realizing the Aadhaar field should cap input at 4 characters, and that controlled inputs trimming on every keystroke would break typing names with spaces — I need local draft state per field that commits on blur instead.\n\nKeying the input component on docType+field keeps it stable across re-renders while sample switches naturally remount the step. I'm fine with blur committing before a \"Next\" click, and the owner-names textarea splitting on blur too — unconfirmed fields won't block navigation since the engine already flags low-confidence results.\n\nFor the questions step, I'm laying out yes/no toggle buttons with provisional badges and a counter, wiring the final submit to POST relevant answers and transition into the report step. The overall flow is five steps — scheme, docs, confirm, questions, report — with a shared header showing app name, step count, language toggle, and a back button, plus scrolling to top on each transition.\n\nI'm writing a client-side utility that downsizes an uploaded image to a JPEG data URL, capping its longest side and compressing quality before upload.\n\nSince createImageBitmap can fail on HEIC files, I need to catch that and surface the aiFailed error message. I also want to avoid unnecessary effects given stricter React hooks lint rules, so I'll set the document language directly inside the toggle's event handler rather than in a useEffect. Now I'm double-checking how page.tsx behaves with cacheComponents enabled on the server side.\n\nThe page just imports static data like listSchemes and SAMPLES, which are serializable, including the Question provenance objects, so that's fine. For the client component, I'm passing catalog and samples as typed props, using type-only imports so they get erased at build time, and making sure accessibility basics are covered—minimum tap target sizes, visible focus rings, aria-live regions for status updates, and setting the document's lang attribute appropriately. I'm also mapping tone categories to Tailwind color classes for green, red, amber, and zinc states, keeping dark mode styling consistent with the global background.\n\nI'm deciding to simplify by forcing a light-only theme rather than dealing with dark mode contrast issues—setting color-scheme to light and removing the dark media query, using explicit white backgrounds with dark text on cards for a cleaner, more consistent demo look. I'm also checking that the sample docs have high enough confidence scores that the confirm step won't show unnecessary highlights, which is acceptable for now, and I'm planning to add scheme name and cover sheet details to the report component for printing.\n\nFor the print-only cover sheet, I'm keeping it minimal—just title, scheme name, date, verdicts, a list of added documents with masked Aadhaar numbers, fix/verify items, and a disclaimer, deliberately avoiding extra personal fields beyond the masked data.\n\nStarting to scaffold the main app component with shared UI pieces and state management for the step flow through scheme selection, docs, confirm, questions, and report.\n\nSetting up state hooks for scheme ID, extracted docs, answers, sample ID, report, and a loading/error status, plus a navigation helper that scrolls to top on step change and an async check function that posts to the API and moves to the report step once results come back.\n\nNow I'm building the sticky header with a logo button that resets the flow and a language toggle button.\n\nBelow that goes a step indicator showing progress out of five steps, plus a demo badge if a sample was used, and the scheme step shows the app's tagline and disclaimer.\n\nI'm designing the photo upload handler for each document slot—converting the image to JPEG, posting it to the extraction API, and handling three outcomes: success (merge the doc), AI unavailable (turn off AI mode and fall back to a blank doc with a notice), or failure (same notice path). I'll validate the returned doc client-side with the schema as a safety check even though the server already validates it.\n\nWhen AI is off, I want to hide the photo button and just show the unavailable banner instead of a disabled control. I'm also deciding not to clear the demo label when a user adds a real photo on top of a sample—only \"Start over\" should reset that, keeping the logic simpler.\n\nNext I still need to build the confirm step with field cards, the questions step, and the full report view, so I'll start writing the UI file now.\n\nAll 148 tests pass now — the issue was an arrow function in beforeEach returning the mock, which Vitest treated as teardown; switching to a block body fixed it. Moving on to the UI, starting with fonts and global styles before the client components.\n\n"}}
