{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:45:37.548Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now I'm planning the client-side UI architecture: a server component page that builds the catalog from listSchemes() and sample data, then passes it down to a KaagazApp client component as plain props so it can prerender statically.\n\nThis client app holds state for language, step, scheme, docs, answers, and report, split into separate step components (scheme, docs, confirm, questions, report) kept small to stay under file size limits. The client only imports pure logic and types, never the evaluate/schemes registry, keeping that work server-side and the bundle lean.\n\nFor the report step, I'm having it POST to /api/check on entry and render the demo label, eligibility and readiness banners, a visible \"check at the bank\" list for external checks, separate benefit shares, and a \"fix before you apply\" section listing required actions.\n\nI also need a \"please verify\" section, a collapsed \"OK\" details block, supporting docs, and a print cover sheet triggered by window.print() using Tailwind's print-only visibility classes, showing scheme, date, masked Aadhaar, verdicts, and fixes. The footer must always show both Assamese and English disclaimers regardless of language, messages should use the pick(lang, ...) helper, and Aadhaar masking relies on the pure maskAadhaar utility.\n\nFor the docs step, each required/optional slot gets a card with status and buttons to capture a photo via file input or manually type in document details, which upserts a blank doc entry into the list.\n\nActually, the cleaner flow is to let the docs step just add documents (either photo-extracted or manual blank), and have the confirm step handle all editing and field display, highlighting low-confidence extractions. If the AI service is unavailable during photo upload, the server returns a 503 and I show an \"AI unavailable\" message while automatically falling back to a manual blank doc entry. I'm now considering whether to check AI availability at page load via a server component reading process.env, but with Next 16's cacheComponents this value could get baked in at build time rather than reflecting runtime config.\n\nRather than trying to detect AI availability upfront, it's simpler to just handle it reactively through the 503 response at runtime. As a nice touch, I could track an `aiOff` state once that error occurs and show a persistent notice at the top while still leaving the photo upload buttons visible. For the docs step, I'm adding a \"try a sample\" option that shows samples relevant to the selected scheme (A/B for pm-kisan, C for widow), where clicking a sample populates both the documents and answers from that sample data and takes the user to the confirm step with everything already at high confidence.\n\nI'll keep sampleId tracked but reset it only on start over or scheme change, since editing after using a sample should still keep the demo-mode label since the data is fake-derived. For language toggling, I'm putting the toggle in the header alongside the app name and step indicator, and syncing document.documentElement.lang via an effect. I'm also planning simple step navigation with Back/Next buttons, scrolling to top on step change, and focusing each step's heading for accessibility.\n\nSince newer eslint-hooks rules flag synchronous setState inside effects, I should avoid fetching the report inside a useEffect and instead trigger the fetch directly from the \"See the result\" button handler -- setting the step, resetting the report, flipping loading on, then performing the fetch and updating state from there.\n\nFor the Questions step I'm planning Yes/No toggle buttons with aria-pressed, a provisional badge when a question's provenance is marked provisional, and a counter for answered questions, using the language picker helper for text.\n\nFor the Confirm step I'm realizing there's a bug: trimming the field value on every keystroke would strip trailing spaces immediately, making it impossible to type a name with a space like \"Ramesh Kumar\" since the space disappears before the next letter is typed -- I need to only trim on blur or submit, not on each change.\n\nTo fix this I'm giving each field its own local text state that initializes from the document's current value and updates freely as the user types, committing the trimmed value to the parent either on blur or on every change (since committing on change while still displaying the untrimmed local text works fine too, and is simpler than worrying about blur timing on mobile taps).\n\nFor the editable fields I'm wiring up specific input behaviors: date hint formatting for DOB, numeric input modes with length limits for Aadhaar and account number, and auto-capitalization for IFSC, plus a masked \"XXXX XXXX 4821\" style display for Aadhaar. Editing a field clears its confidence flag since a manual edit counts as confirmation, and I'm letting the user proceed even with unconfirmed fields since the engine already flags low-confidence entries as needing verification — keeping things honest rather than blocking progress, while making sure each component file stays small.\n\nNow I'm planning the file breakdown: a main app component handling state, header, and step routing, then separate step components for scheme selection, document upload, confirmation, questions, and the final report (plus supporting report pieces like check items and cover sheet). I'm working out the type boundaries so the client only imports type-only scheme and sample definitions while the server page handles the full catalog and schema registry, and I'm starting to write the server page component that wires the catalog data into the client app.\n\nI also note the catalog gets baked in at build time since the page is statically prerendered, which matters for later runtime scheme registration but isn't this task's concern. Now I'm sketching the step state machine itself — defining the step union type, the step order, and the shape of state covering language, current step, selected scheme, extracted docs, answers, sample selection, and report status.\n\nI'm working through the key actions: switching schemes resets downstream state and advances to docs; loading a sample populates scheme, docs, answers, and sample id then jumps to confirm; running the check moves to the report step, sets loading status, and posts to the check API with scheme id/version, docs, and only the relevant answers, letting the server determine today's date in IST rather than passing it explicitly.\n\nI need to guard against stale report responses if the user navigates away before the fetch resolves, so I'll track requests with a counter ref and verify it before applying state. For the UI, I'm planning a header with the app name in both English and Bengali, a tagline, a language toggle, and a step indicator showing progress out of five steps, plus focus management that shifts attention and scrolls to the top heading whenever the step changes.\n\nI'm centralizing the step heading into a single shared element that pulls its title from a map rather than having each step render its own, and syncing the document's lang attribute to the chosen language whenever it changes. I'm also setting up the photo upload handler for the docs step, which sets a busy state and clears notices before processing the image.\n\nConverting the file to a JPEG data URL, posting it to the extraction endpoint, and validating the response against the expected schema -- if extraction succeeds I attach the parsed doc, otherwise I fall back to a blank doc and surface either an \"AI unavailable\" or generic failure notice, flagging AI as off when relevant, with the busy state cleared in all cases.\n\nI'm deciding to keep the sampleId persistent until the user restarts or changes schemes rather than trying to track whether every doc is still a sample -- too fiddly otherwise. I also want to lift the AI-off notice state up to the parent component so it survives even if DocsStep unmounts when navigating between steps.\n\nFor the hidden file input, I'm building a label-wrapped input with capture set to the environment camera, hidden visually but triggering the photo picker, and resetting its value after reading so the same file can be reselected. Samples should filter by scheme id so Orunodoi hides the section since it has no sample, but I'm reconsidering whether to show all three sample buttons even though sample C belongs to the widow scheme and would switch schemes if tapped from the PM-KISAN docs page.\n\nI'm deciding to just show the samples relevant to whichever scheme is active, so the demo path stays clean. Then for ConfirmStep I'm planning a per-document field card, and for ReportStep I'm structuring the CheckItem component with the message, a fix-where definition list, a source link with title and verified date (or a no-source-link fallback), and a provisional badge when needed, plus a compact external-checks list pulled from the scheme matched by check id. For the benefit card I'm laying out central share, state top-up, total, period, instalments, and an override note, noting Orunodoi's central share would render as ₹0.\n\nNow I'm working out the print-only cover sheet — hidden on screen, visible in print — showing title, scheme name, date, rule version, verdicts, and added documents reduced to just name plus masked identifiers (aadhaar and account number masked to last four digits), along with the fix/verify lists and bilingual disclaimer, while making the interactive report UI hidden in print. I'm leaning toward just marking the main app print:hidden and rendering the CoverSheet separately within ReportStep.\n\nSince the ReportStep itself is wrapped in a print:hidden container, I realize the CoverSheet needs to be rendered as a sibling outside that wrapper rather than nested inside it, so it stays visible when printing. I'm also adding the bilingual disclaimer footer to both the report and cover sheet, a \"Start over\" button, and accessibility touches like aria-pressed on yes/no buttons, aria-labels on verdict sections, and a live-region status for loading states.\n\nI'm also checking which eslint and jsx-a11y rules from Next's config are relevant here, including the newer react-hooks/react-compiler rules, though most don't apply to this component.\n\nFor the Field component, I'll initialize local state from the prop using a lazy initializer and reset it via a key change. Now I'm ready to write the KaagazApp component itself.\n\nTests pass, layout/font/shared UI are done. Now moving to client components, keeping engine logic server-side—browser only imports `flow`, `reportView`, `i18n`, and types, with decisions coming from `/api/check`.\n\n"}}
